"Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "blocking", "title": "A real password whose first piece before an unencoded @ is a placeholder form passes check-pr-hygiene when a /, ? or # follows that @, contrary to the owner-ruled bound on the exemption's residual risk", "recommendation": "Reproduce by reading `_has_real_url_password` against `https://admin:ab@Xk9/mQ2vLr7@host` (or `postgres://app:password@Kx9fPq?2mZ@db`). `_URL_CREDENTIAL` cuts the password at the first `@` (`ab`), `_URL_AUTHORITY` (`://[^/?#\\s]*`) stops at the `/` and so sees only one `@`, and `_is_placeholder_password('admin', 'ab')` is true because the piece is three characters or fewer. `credential_shape` returns None, and the PR gate and the new exit-9 guard both pass a random-looking secret. The owner's 2026-11-24 ruling bounds the accepted risk with 'a mixed-case secret in a wrapper, or one holding an unencoded `@`, still refuses'. The plan's Constraint says 'A random-looking string must still be reported'. `plugin/docs/waivers.md` says 'a password holding an unencoded `@` before the host, still refuses'. All three are false for this shape. The `/` stop dates from 9beca39e, and bb6edaf6 widened the gap to `?` and `#`. Why it broke: a placeholder followed by `@host/path?q=a@b` and a password `pw@X/Y@host` have the same syntax, and the code settles the ambiguity in the exempt direction. Scope: class. Two ways to close it. One is to settle the ambiguity toward reporting, so any further `@` in the same URL token (to whitespace or a quote) disqualifies the exemption. That brings back the email-in-query false positive bb6edaf6 removed, but a waiver answers that. The other is to keep the behaviour and get the owner's ruling on the wider risk. Either way the ruling, the plan's Tradeoffs and waivers.md must then state what the code does. Add a positive control in `test_a_real_looking_url_password_is_still_a_credential` for the `/`, `?` and `#` variants.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/docs/waivers.md", "tests/test_pr_gate.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A url-embedded password whose piece before its first unencoded @ passes _is_placeholder_password, and which carries a /, ? or # after that @ and before the next @.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py:_has_real_url_password / _URL_AUTHORITY, path separator '/'"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py:_has_real_url_password / _URL_AUTHORITY, query '?'"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py:_has_real_url_password / _URL_AUTHORITY, fragment '#'"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The new cumulative dispatch refusal names no expected yield, and the plan carries no disposition against the proportionality norm", "recommendation": "Instance: the decision record for the new control is missing one statement. The `critic-dispatch-credential-shape` guard that `begin_review` adds is a new pre-dispatch control. `nonfunctional-requirements.md` ## Direction says \"Adding a control names the yield it expects **and emits that yield observably**\". The emission half is met, since the guard writes one `guard-refusal` fact. The plan never names the expected yield, though. Its `governed_by:` lists only api-contract and architecture. The sibling guard plans that added controls (`build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md`, `build-plan-boundary-coverage-check.md`) each carry a `nonfunctional-requirements` disposition stating it: \"Expected yield: rare. The store scan found 4 of 180 ...\". The Constraints line (\"it moves one that was going to happen\") says what one firing buys. It does not say how often the guard is expected to fire, or what record would justify retiring it. The consequence: when the Norm Health yield query is built, this guard has no stated expectation to be measured against, so it can only be defended on principle, which is the failure the norm names. Fix: add a `nonfunctional-requirements` entry to the plan's `governed_by:` with the proportionality disposition. It should state the expected rate (after Chunk 01, the PR gate's non-placeholder credential refusals, which `evidence list --kind guard-refusal` can compare between guard `pr-hygiene` and guard `critic-dispatch-credential-shape`). It should also state the evidence that would retire the guard (for example, no firings over a stated window while `pr-hygiene` credential refusals stay at zero). This touches no code and needs no suite run. Whether the omission is a norm departure belongs to Goal 3; this finding rates only the missing record of the decision.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A pre-dispatch refusal added on this branch whose plan states no expected yield under the proportionality norm.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py begin_review CREDENTIAL GUARD (CREDENTIAL_GUARD)"}], "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 1, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The second-@ test over-reports a placeholder when a compact one-line structure follows the URL without whitespace", "recommendation": "`_URL_AUTHORITY` stops only at `/?#` and whitespace. In `{\"dsn\":\"postgres://u:password@db\",\"owner\":\"a@b.com\"}` the authority runs on through the quote and comma to the email's `@`, so the placeholder is reported. This fails toward reporting, the base reported it too, and a waiver answers it. No change is needed unless the fix for the blocking finding redraws where the URL token ends: a quote, comma or semicolon would be the natural stop.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Some mask forms are untested", "recommendation": "`_PLACEHOLDER_PASSWORD_SHAPES` exempts `\u2026` and `[REDACTED]`, and the placeholder words `passwd`, `changeme`, `dummy`, `fake` and `test`, with no parametrized case. No assertion pins those exemptions, so a later narrowing of the mask regex would go unnoticed.", "files": ["tests/test_pr_gate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The url-password placeholder exemption is special-cased by name inside the table-driven credential_shape loop", "recommendation": "_SECRET_SHAPES already carries per-shape credential policy (line_form, is_credential), and the cloud-token placeholder is expressed there. The url-password placeholder test now sits outside the table as `if name == _URL_PASSWORD and not _has_real_url_password(line)`, so the table reads as if url-embedded passwords are reported unconditionally. Only two shapes need this and the special case is local, so no change is needed now. If a third shape needs a placeholder test, replace the `is_credential` bool with a per-shape predicate rather than adding a second `if name ==` branch. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "_has_real_url_password finds the authority by a hard-coded offset tied to the credential regex's lookbehind", "recommendation": "`_URL_AUTHORITY.match(line, m.start() - 3)` works only because `_URL_CREDENTIAL`'s lookbehind is exactly `://`, so the two regexes are coupled by the literal 3. This is correct today and the test suite covers the `@`-past-host cases. If either regex changes, derive the offset from the shared token (e.g. `len(\"://\")`) or capture the authority in a single pattern. No edit is wanted for this branch.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The credential guard's refusal reason slug is an inline literal, not a declared constant", "recommendation": "evidence.append_guard_refusal says `reason` comes from a closed set the control declares, and the stale-evidence guard declares its set (STALE_EVIDENCE_REASONS). The new guard names its `guard` as a constant (CREDENTIAL_GUARD) but passes `reason=\"credential-shape\"` as a literal, and the test pins that literal too. Nothing reads the slug from a constant today, so nothing goes wrong. Declaring it next to CREDENTIAL_GUARD would keep the guard's two machine keys in one home.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: the placeholder survey figures cite no command that re-derives them", "recommendation": "Records Pass over files_oracle (`api-contract.md`, `build-plan-credential-placeholders.md`, `data-model.md`, `changes/credential-placeholders.md`, `owner-rulings.md`). The plan's Tradeoffs give \"of 204 url-embedded password matches, the rule exempts 176 ... keeps 28\". The figures were re-measured after the single-case-name narrowing (they were 202/181/21 before it), but not after bb6edaf6 changed the authority rule. No script or command is cited that would re-derive them. This clears no bar: the record does not ship, and nobody acts on the exact figure. The authoring rule \"A spike that discards its code leaves its numbers unfalsifiable\" applies. No edit is wanted for this branch. Everything else in the oracle set was checked against the code and holds: the exit-9 Error Model entry and the list of what `--force` overrides (exit 7 is skipped under force at `if mode_token == \"verify-resolutions\" and not force`), the data-model's `critic-dispatch-credential-shape` body (`mode`/`branch`/`lines`, reason `credential-shape`, lines never recorded), the change fragment's consumer note, and the owner ruling's exemption set.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-credential-placeholders.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Notes collected before the tree capture are dropped on its two error returns", "recommendation": "In `begin_review`, `credential_notes` (like the existing `notes_scope`) is merged into `notes` only after `capture_tree` and the no-HEAD check. Those two error returns carry no `notes`, so a `--force`-past-credentials note or an unchecked-diff note is lost when the dispatch then fails there. This is harmless: the dispatch failed and the PR gate still refuses. It predates this change for `notes_scope`. It is noted only because the CLI's error branch already prints `result['notes']` for the paths that carry them.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-7"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "credential-placeholders", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "391e202c37c3f25ab208a1fd34bd31b73bb72c17", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-credential-placeholders.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/credential-placeholders.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/docs/waivers.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-24T16:12:13Z"}}
